    "lawCode": "Add §195.25, Pen L",
    "lawSection": "Penal Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S2371\n\nSPONSOR: KAMINSKY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the penal law, in relation to making false statements to\ndistrict attorneys, assistant district attorneys or district attorney\ninvestigators in this state\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThis bill establishes the crime of making a false statement to a\nDistrict Attorney or District Attorney investigator.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1: Establishes the class E felony of making a false statement to\na District Attorney or District Attorney investigator\n\nSection 2: Provides that this act shall take effect immediately.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nFederal law prohibits lying to federal law enforcement officers. Such\nlies can result in federal felony charges, and have in high profile\ncases such as those of Bernie Madoff, Michael Flynn, and Martha Stewart.\nLying to law enforcement can obstruct investigations and protect\nsuspects' business associates, while the knowledge that lying to a law\nenforcement agent is a crime also leads to truthful interviews, which\nform the bases of investigations. The provision of federal law that\nestablishes this crime, 18 U.S.C. § 1001, has proven to be an effective\ntool in fighting white collar crime. In cases where it can be difficult\nto establish proof of embezzlement, fraud, or other white collar crimes,\nlies to federal law enforcement can be bad actors' undoing.\n\nThis tool is not currently available to state law enforcement officers.\nNew York is the home of one of the most powerful financial centers in\nthe world, and in its shadow lie plenty of people ready and willing to\ntake advantage of innocent, unsuspecting citizens and constituents. By\nestablishing a state crime for lying to law enforcement in narrow\ncircumstances, prosecutors will be enabled to seek justice from these\ncriminals.\n\nIt is important to bear in mind the differences between state and feder-\nal law, and to consider that some common crimes under the Penal Law,\nincluding domestic violence crimes, are rarely prosecuted under federal\nlaw. Thus, this bill limits the crime of making a false, material state-\nment to a District Attorney or District Attorney investigator to non-\nviolent, white collar crimes. The bill also requires that the District\nAttorney internally declare his or her intent to investigate the case as\na qualifying crime, to prevent this crime from inadvertently being\ncharged in non-white collar cases. These safeguards will ensure that\nthis statute remains a powerful tool for prosecutors, while ensuring\nthat it is not used in inappropriate circumstances.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2019: New bill\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
